 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Ghosting Artifacts:** Pixel-aligned methods generate redundant primitives in overlapping regions. Using a global volume and filtering duplicates addresses this [S5].
-- **Depth Noise:** Monocular depth predictions contain noise and floating artifacts. Statistical filtering is required to remove floaters [S5].
-- **Recursive Convergence:** The position offset $\Delta \mu_i$ depends on the previous estimation. Failure to iterate or initialize $\Delta \mu^{prev}_i$ correctly can lead to inaccurate geometry queries [S5].
+*   **Redundancy and Ghosting:** Pixel-aligned methods generate redundant and inconsistent primitives in overlapping regions, leading to ghosting artifacts. This method addresses this by using a global volume and filtering duplicates via uniform and statistical filters [S2].
+*   **Depth Noise:** Monocular depth predictions contain noise and floating artifacts. The statistical filter is crucial to remove floaters after uniform filtering [S2].
+*   **Recursive Convergence:** The position offset $\Delta \mu_i$ depends on the previous estimation. If not converged, opacity and covariance may be queried at inaccurate positions. The method empirically finds that two recursive iterations are sufficient for convergence during inference [S2].
 
